  "notes": "The German \"hands up\" production board — Yann Peifer and Manuel Reuter — behind the section's biggest hands-up act plus a pseudonym factory, with a defining window of 2004-2013. The act itself is written up in ARTISTS as Cascada; this model is the room, and where they differ is the board's wider brief. The tempo is the documented hands-up band, 138-155, which runs higher than the act's own 138-150 because \"from the mid-2000s the board imported jumpstyle and hardstyle elements, which shows as a distorted, pitch-swept kick and a reverse-bass feel on the harder aliases\" — hence a kick allowed a fifth hit, a reverse perc lane and the widest pitch walk on the roll here. The documented signature is a bass instruction: **\"heavy basslines placed on the off-beat\"**, so the bassline is offbeat eighths sitting loud rather than a kick mirror, and \"supersaw synthesiser leads\" are the documented lead timbre with \"the lead doubling the topline at unison or an octave up so the instrumental and vocal hooks are the same line\" — an octave-echo counter at a 0.88 density. \"Hard kicks, clipped short so they read at 145+, a layered clap on 2&4, offbeat open hats, 16th closed hats, and long snare-roll builds\" supplies the rest. The board's method is industrial — one house style, many aliases, an owned label and a cover-version pipeline — plus a documented habit of shipping a slow piano \"Candlelight Mix\" alongside every club mix, which is named here rather than authored as a mode, since the compendium's multi-mode table does not list this board. ⚠ The exact placements are hands-up/Eurodance convention by the entry's own statement; the velocities, densities and registers are INFERRED.",
